\section{A Brief Glossary}\label{ch1-a-brief-glossary}

A few terms recur throughout the manual and are worth defining once. A
\emph{feature tag} is one of the empty structs like
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}Pipeline} that you pass to
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}DSL}.

A \emph{mixin} is the nested
\texttt{Mixin\textless{}\allowbreak{}Derived\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
template inside a feature tag; it is where the feature's methods
actually live. A \emph{clause} is one arm of a
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}match} table, built with
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}when} or
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}otherwise}.

An \emph{NTTP} is a non-type template parameter --- a value, as opposed
to a type, passed between angle brackets.
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}leaf\textless{}\allowbreak{}"num"\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
and
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}pattern\textless{}\allowbreak{}"[0-9]+"\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
both use NTTPs. A \emph{fixpoint} is the state a rewrite pass reaches
when no rule fires any more; Chapter 14 covers this in detail.

\section{Expectations and Scope}\label{ch1-expectations-and-scope}

MuDSL values clarity and composability above completeness. The pattern
engine implements a useful subset, not full POSIX regex. The parser
combinators are a teaching-quality Parsec, not an industrial parser
toolkit. The PEG engine is additive and lightweight.

Within its chosen scope, however, each piece is consistent, documented,
and designed to combine with the others. Understanding the design
philosophy in this chapter will make the concrete APIs in later chapters
feel predictable rather than arbitrary.

\section{Summary}\label{ch1-summary}

MuDSL is a header-only C++20 toolkit for embedded DSLs, built on CRTP,
feature mixins, and NTTP strings. You compose features by inheriting
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}DSL}, and the compiler does all
the polymorphic work. The library favours value semantics, deferred
evaluation, explicit error flow, and operator-based composition, and it
grows additively rather than by rewriting itself.

The next chapter turns philosophy into practice. You will obtain the
header, compile a first program with and without CMake, run the bundled
examples, and learn to read the most common compile errors. From there,
Chapter 3 opens the \texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}DSL} base
class and shows exactly how one line of inheritance becomes a fully
featured language.
